macOS SDK for cross-compilation
================================

The C++ macOS build (`./build.sh` → noctalum-cpp-macos image) uses osxcross
to link against Apple frameworks from a Linux host.  osxcross needs a real
macOS SDK.  Apple's SDK Agreement forbids redistributing it, so this
directory is intentionally empty in git — you extract the SDK yourself
from a Mac that you own and drop the tarball here.

What to drop here
-----------------
One file: `MacOSX<version>.sdk.tar.xz`.  For example:

    MacOSX14.4.sdk.tar.xz
    MacOSX13.3.sdk.tar.xz

The build picks the highest-numbered `MacOSX*.sdk.tar.xz` it finds.  Any
SDK from Xcode 11 or newer works; newer is better (later deployment target
support).  The build pins the deployment target to macOS 11.0, so the
resulting .app runs on Big Sur and newer.

How to package the SDK
----------------------
On the Mac (needs Xcode or the Command Line Tools installed):

    # 1. Clone the osxcross helper repo (matches the version baked into
    #    our Docker image).
    git clone https://github.com/tpoechtrager/osxcross ~/osxcross

    # 2. Package the SDK from the currently-selected Xcode.
    cd ~/osxcross
    ./tools/gen_sdk_package.sh

    # 3. Copy the produced MacOSX<version>.sdk.tar.xz over to this
    #    directory on your build host.
    scp MacOSX*.sdk.tar.xz your-build-host:/path/to/noctalum/cpp/third_party/osxcross-sdk/

If Xcode isn't installed (Command Line Tools only), use
`./tools/gen_sdk_package_pbzx.sh` from the same repo — same output.

Skipping the macOS build
------------------------
If you don't have (or don't want to package) a Mac SDK, run

    ./build.sh --cpp-no-macos

and macOS artifacts will not be produced.  Deploy still succeeds; the
Downloads page just shows "not available" on the macOS cards.
